WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.640
‘Going for an Indian’
or ‘having a curry’

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.400
is almost as stereotypically British
as roast dinners or fish and chips.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.440
There are around 12,000
curry houses in Britain.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.960
The word came from the Tamil ‘kari’,
which meant a spiced sauce.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.840
But gradually the term was
adapted and used as a generic term

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.000
for any stew-like food
from the Indian subcontinent -

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.840
rather ignoring such subtleties as,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.120
regional differences
and completely different flavours,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.440
textures, cooking methods
and ingredients.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.120
The first definite mention
of ‘curry’ in English is in 1598.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.520
But the first recipe for curry
published in Britain

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.360
wasn’t until 1747,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.000
by which time Brits,
long-time traders with India,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.560
were slowly taking over the country.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.480
Thousands of British men and women
spent time in India.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.120
They had Indian cooks and servants,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.680
and while some tried to maintain
Western eating habits,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.080
most quickly embraced
the tastes of their new home.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.440
When they returned to Britain,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.960
they brought their new love
of Indian food back with them.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.240
Those who had lived in India

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.560
knew very well that not all
Indian dishes were curry,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.520
and when the first,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.720
albeit short-lived
Indian restaurant in Britain

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.680
opened in London in 1810,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.380
its menu contained khichdi, chutnee and pulao...

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.440
dishes later known by the anglicised
names, kedgeree, chutney and pilaf.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.320
Manuscript books,
kept by those in the know,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.560
also differentiated between dishes.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.600
But they were very much
a minority, and in Britain,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.240
curry became a catch-all term for
almost anything with Indian spices.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.320
Slowly certain dishes,
especially chicken curry -

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.800
which used an elderly fowl,
which had stopped laying eggs -

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.520
entered the mainstream repertoire.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.440
Ready-made curry powders
were widely sold.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.520
British palates
were not used to Indian spices,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.720
and the early recipes are more
like gently flavoured meaty stews,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.000
laden with turmeric, ginger and
galangale, with cayenne for a hit.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.520
By the 19th Century,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.560
curry was in every cookbook,
mainly as a leftover dish.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.000
The Anglo-Indian cuisine
of this era was a hybrid,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.320
using pickled cucumbers to replace
mango, apple instead of tamarind,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.520
and ready-made spice blends galore.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.840
It was great, but had very little
in common with its Eastern roots.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.720
Queen Victoria
took a different approach,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.560
regularly eating ‘Indian dishes’

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.880
prepared by the cook
to her Indian attendants,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.200
who’d joined the royal staff
at her Golden Jubilee in 1887.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.600
There were a few eating houses
run by Indians,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.840
mainly for other Indians,
in port towns,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.360
but it took until the 1920s
for high profile restaurants to open,

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.920
catering for a British market.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.200
By 1946 there were around
20 Indian restaurants in London.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.360
Boomtime for curry
came after the Second World War,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.120
when the partition of India
brought migrants

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.720
from Punjab and Sylhet to Britain.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.080
In the 1970s,

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.240
civil war in Bangladesh saw
many Bangladeshis flee to Britain,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.920
and even today many
apparently generic Indian restaurants

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.200
are really Bangladeshi.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.240
Curry, in its 1970s form,
was cheap and cheerful,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.880
adapted for British tastes.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.240
In 2001 the then foreign secretary,
Robin Cook

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.760
declared boldly that Britain’s national dish was chicken tikka masala…

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.960
a classic example of an
Indian dish - buttered chicken -

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.440
meeting British tastes,

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.000
in this case with the addition
of cream and, allegedly,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.440
cream of tomato soup.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.240
In the last decade or so,

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.680
the British relationship
to Indian food has changed.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.040
Most of us have grown out
of wanting something so hot

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.320
it’ll hospitalise us.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.160
Leading Indian chefs are teaching us

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.160
that there is so
much more to Indian food

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.040
than the comforting predictability
of the average restaurant menu.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.560
Maybe after 250 years,
we’ve simply come full circle.

